{
  "id": "disaster-resilience",
  "title": "Disaster mitigation in 20 high-risk US counties",
  "summary": "Natural-hazard mitigation programs in 20 US counties with high expected yearly disaster losses (at most two per state), from FEMA's National Risk Index. Returns follow the National Institute of Building Sciences' finding that federal mitigation grants save about $6 per $1. Program sizes and diminishing returns are illustrative assumptions.",
  "valueUnit": "dollars of losses avoided per $1,000 (lens-weighted)",
  "sources": [
    {
      "name": "FEMA — National Risk Index v1.20 (December 2025), county table",
      "url": "https://www.fema.gov/about/openfema/data-sets/national-risk-index-data"
    },
    {
      "name": "National Institute of Building Sciences — Natural Hazard Mitigation Saves: 2019 Report",
      "url": "https://nibs.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/NIBS_MMC_MitigationSaves_2019.pdf"
    }
  ],
  "assumptions": {
    "program_share_of_covered_eal": 0.005,
    "nibs_federal_grant_bcr": {
      "ifld": 7.0,
      "hrcn": 5.0,
      "trnd": 5.0,
      "erqk": 3.0,
      "wfir": 3.0
    },
    "curve_multiples_first_middle_last": [
      1.6,
      1.0,
      0.4
    ],
    "note": "Counties: the 20 with the highest total expected annual loss (EAL) in FEMA's National Risk Index v1.20 (Dec 2025), at most 2 per state, skipping any without vulnerability or resilience scores. Program size (cap): 0.5% of one year of the county's EAL from the hazards mitigated (inland flooding, hurricane, tornado, earthquake, wildfire), rounded to $10,000; an illustrative sizing, not a sourced cost estimate. Average return: each county's mix of those hazards, weighted by their dollar EAL, applied to the NIBS 2019 federal-grant benefit-cost ratios (flood 7:1, wind 5:1, earthquake 3:1, wildfire 3:1). Other hazards (coastal flooding, heat, drought, etc.) are left out because NIBS reports no grant ratio for them. Diminishing returns: best projects first, so the first dollar returns 1.6x the county average, the midpoint 1.0x and the last 0.4x, averaging to the NIBS ratio. Lens weights: Social vulnerability multiplies by NRI SoVI score / 50; Low resilience by (100 - NRI resilience score) / 50 (both scores are national percentiles, so a median county counts 1:1). EAL includes NRI's dollar value of expected deaths and injuries."
  },
